meta {
  name: Connector Credentials - Create
  type: http
  seq: 26
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/v1/connector-credentials
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{apiKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "connector_id": {{connectorId}},
    "name": "Acme production key",
    "data": {
      "api_key": "replace-me"
    },
    "expires_at": null
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 201) {
    const credential = res.body.data?.connector_credential;
    if (credential && credential.id) {
      bru.setVar("connectorCredentialId", credential.id);
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Attaches a credential to the key's workspace without a browser — what the
  `connectors:manage` ability was defined for. A platform that creates a
  workspace per customer needs to attach that customer's API keys
  programmatically; doing it by hand in the UI doesn't scale.

  Stores the new ID in `connectorCredentialId`.

  - `connector_id` — required, must exist. Get it from `Connectors - List`
    (the internal `Connectors` folder sets `connectorId` for you).
  - `name` — required, max 255.
  - `data` — required array, the connector's own credential fields.
  - `expires_at` — optional, nullable date.

  OAuth connectors are refused here with a `422`: their credentials come
  from an authorization redirect only a browser can complete, and accepting
  hand-written token data would produce a credential that silently can't
  refresh itself.

  `created_by` stays null on credentials made this way — an API key belongs
  to a workspace, not a person.

  Response is wrapped:
  `{ success, statusCode, message, data: { connector_credential } }`.
}
